speechtext:
The Government is very seriously .considering the whole question, and before the session ends it may be able to lay some proposals before the House. I have nothing to speak of about that now. The hon. member for Edmonton carried on a discussion with reference to this matter, and traversed ground which was interesting. I listened to his remarks very carefully. We have been a long time in debate on this matter and I do not care to traverse the ground again, but I do not want my hon. friend to think that I was not
	{
interested in the remarks he made. With some of them I agree. I think that more careful examination into some of the others would scarcely lead me along the same line as my hon. friend.

I really think that during the war, when so many civil servants are leaving the departments and going to 119ft}
the front, it ought to have been possible to get along without any additional buildings. With the exercise of reasonable economy I think that would have been possible. I can understand that in times of peace, when the country is prosperous, the Government might like to give the civil servants as much room .as possible, perhaps to save the necessity of two clerks occuping one room	giving each a
separate apartment. But why it should be necessary, during this period of stress, to rent II additional buildings I am at a loss to understand. [DOT]I think on reflection the minister must see that the desire to rent buildings from friends who may have had them to rent has been more potent with the Government than the necessity for increased accommodation.
